<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khambhat</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cambay</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Khambhat, historically Cambay, is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, at the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-khambhat">Gulf of Khambhat</a>. From the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> it was one of the great ports of western India, exporting textiles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>, and it was visited by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> and many later European travellers. Silting of the gulf later ruined its harbour, and from about 1730 it was the seat of the Nawabs of Cambay.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khambhat.jpg" alt="Khambhat" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dhruv855 · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:City_of_Khambaht(Tower_Area).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
